{"c": null, "r": "I'm designing a Bank class where each instance maintains its own accounts dictionary. The open_account method validates that the account doesn't already exist and the initial balance is non-negative, raising ValueError if either check fails. For deposits, I need to validate that the name exists first—raising KeyError if it doesn't—and that the amount is positive, raising ValueError otherwise, with the order of validation mattering for edge cases like testing with unknown names and zero amounts.

Withdraw validates the name exists, amount is positive, and sufficient funds are available before deducting. Transfer checks that both source and destination accounts exist, the amount is positive, and the accounts are different, with validation happening before any balance changes. I should also consider whether to enforce integer amounts using isinstance checks, since the task specifies amounts are integers, though I need to be careful about type hints versus runtime validation.
", "t": [["write_file", "{"path":"D:\\Projects\\local_ai\\strata-lab\\runs\\work\\hermes\\bank-1794053343\\bank.py","content":"\"\"\"Account helpers. Balances are integer cents.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass InsufficientFunds(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when a withdrawal or transfer exceeds the source balance.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass Bank:\n    \"\"\"A set of named accounts with integer-cent balances.\n\n    Each Bank owns its own accounts; two instances never share them.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._accounts = {}\n\n    def open_account(self, name, initial=0):\n        if initial < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"initial balance must be >= 0: \" + str(initial))\n        if name in self._accounts:\n            raise ValueError(\"account exists: \" + str(name))\n        self._accounts[name] = initial\n\n    def deposit(self, name, amount):\n        if name not in self._accounts:\n            raise KeyError(name)\n        if amount <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"amount must be > 0: \" + str(amount))\n        self._accounts[name] += amount\n\n    def withdraw(self, name, amount):\n        self._require_account(name)\n        if amount <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"amount must be > 0: \" + str(amount))\n        current = self._accounts[name]\n        if current < amount:\n            raise InsufficientFunds(\n                \"balance \" + str(current) + \" < amount \" + str(amount))\n        self._accounts[name] = current - amount\n\n    def balance(self, name):\n        if name not in self._accounts:\n            raise KeyError(name)\n        return self._accounts[name]\n\n    def transfer(self, src, dst, amount):\n        self._require_account(src)\n        self._require_account(dst)\n        if amount <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"amount must be > 0: \" + str(amount))\n        if src == dst:\n            raise ValueError(\"cannot transfer to the same account: \" + str(src))\n        src_balance = self._accounts[src]\n        if src_balance < amount:\n            raise InsufficientFunds(\n                \"balance \" + str(src_balance) + \" < amount \" + str(amount))\n        self._accounts[src] = src_balance - amount\n        self._accounts[dst] += amount\n\n    def _require_account(self, name):\n        if name not in self._accounts:\n            raise KeyError(name)\n        return self._accounts[name]\n\n\ndefault_bank = Bank()\n\n\ndef open_account(name, initial=0):\n    default_bank.open_account(name, initial)\n\n\ndef deposit(name, amount):\n    default_bank.deposit(name, amount)\n\n\ndef withdraw(name, amount):\n    default_bank.withdraw(name, amount)\n\n\ndef balance(name):\n    return default_bank.balance(name)\n\n\ndef transfer(src, dst, amount):\n    default_bank.transfer(src, dst, amount)\n"}"]]}

